    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 275\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 2835-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the parks, recreation and historic preservation\n      law, in relation to requiring an evaluation of the impacts of  the\n      closure  of  public  access  to  any land owned or operated by the\n      office of parks, recreation and historic preservation\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would amend the Parks, Recreation and Historic  Preservation\nLaw to require the Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation\n(OPRHP)  to  issue  a draft report at least six months in advance of any\nclosure of public access to real property owned,  operated  or  adminis-\ntered  by  OPRHP, conduct public hearings, and deliver a final report to\nthe Governor and the legislature.\n \n  The purported purpose of the bill is to  provide  an  opportunity  for\npublic and legislative review over proposed limitations of public access\nto  OPRHP  property.  The  bill would not apply to seasonal closures and\nclosures related to  weather,  maintenance,  and  capital  improvements.\nHowever,  these exceptions do not mitigate the severe operational impact\nthis bill would have on the administration of OPRHP property.    Specif-\nically, these excertions do not include temporary or permanent limits on\npublic access for routine operational reasons, such as public safety, or\nnon-weather  related  emergencies.  Promoting public access to parks and\nhistoric properties is of paramount importance to OPRHP.   However,  the\nsheer  number  of  decisions that must be made by OPRHP staff on a daily\nbasis, including those made to protect public safety,  would  be  under-\nmined by the bill's requirements.\n \n  As  drafted, the bill is also not limited in scope and would therefore\nrequire a detailed and costly economic impact analysis for  any  limita-\ntion  on public access regardless of the type of property in question or\nthe degree to which public access was limited. Requiring OPRHP to devel-\nop reports, conduct hearings, and issue findings  statements  each  time\nOPRHP needs to redirect or limit access to State park resources is oner-\nous  and unnecessary. Setting aside the fiscal concerns presented by the\nbill, the need for OPRHP to retain the flexibility to make  day  to  day\ndecisions  related to management of OPRHP property constrains me to veto\nthe bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 275,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2018
   }
